Campaign clip-wire-workspace, run 2026-08-06 against a150df8 in an isolated
worktree, over Private/UI/SFlowVizWorkspace.cpp with filter FlowViz.UI. Result:

    SURVIVED  IDENTITY CONTROL                            (correct)
    killed    the workspace never subscribes
    SURVIVED  binding a volume does not push              <-- see THE GAP
    killed    the panel is never told about the binding
    killed    the advisory tracks validity rather than binding
    killed    an unbound push reports success

    killed 4  SURVIVED 2  INVALID 0  UNSCORED 0

Baseline was green before scoring and the identity control survived, so these
verdicts are readable rather than noise.

ONE REAL SURVIVOR, AND IT IS NOT A DEFECT IN SetVolume. The production code
calls PushToVolume() at the end of the bind, exactly as it should. A SURVIVED
arm says THE SUITE CANNOT TELL -- never that the code is wrong. Read the source
before filing any survivor as work; clip-wire-push.README records what happens
when that step is skipped.

THE GAP -- BINDING A VOLUME DOES NOT PUSH.

The arm deletes the PushToVolume() call at the end of SetVolume and leaves the
assignment and the panel disclosure intact. It survives a green suite.

WHY THE EXISTING TEST CANNOT CATCH IT, which is the whole lesson here.
FlowViz.UI.Workspace.VolumeBinding binds a volume whose clip model is still
EMPTY -- it even asserts plane count 0 immediately after SetVolume, as a control
-- and only then presses a preset button. So the bind-time push has nothing to
carry, and the later edit-driven push covers for a bind-time push that never
happened. The ORDERING hides the branch. Nothing is weak about the assertions;
they are simply downstream of the only moment that could have failed.

That control assertion is worth looking at twice. `plane count is 0 right after
SetVolume` is a correct and useful control for the test it belongs to, and it is
also the exact statement a missing bind-time push would produce. The same line
reads as rigour and as the defect, depending on what the model held going in.

WHAT DELETING THE CALL COSTS: a workspace with planes already authored -- a
loaded session, or a case opened before the actor existed -- renders unclipped
until the user touches something. A control that has to be wiggled to take. Same
shape as #26, #40/#41, #42 and #50: the unit is correct, the channel exists, and
nothing drives it at the moment that matters.

CLOSED BY FlowViz.UI.Workspace.BindPushesExistingState, which authors the clip
state BEFORE binding and asserts the volume is already clipped with no further
edit. It asserts the authored DISTANCE and NORMAL, not just the plane count: a
push that fabricated a default plane would satisfy a count assertion. Repo
memory degenerate-data-defeats-assertions.

Tracked as task #60.

THE OTHER SURVIVOR IS THE IDENTITY CONTROL, which is the correct result and not
a gap. Of six arms, one is the control and one is the finding above; the other
four were killed. (The no-extent survivor belongs to clip-wire-push, decided as
an equivalent mutant in #58 -- a different campaign over a different file.)

Arm 1 is the identity control and must SURVIVE. If it ever does not, the harness
is scoring noise and every line above is void.

The rationale lives here rather than at the top of the .txt because
parse_mutants.py splits on `%%` and raises ValueError on any record that is not
exactly three `--`-separated fields. A prose header would abort the campaign.
